# .release-removals — files that used to be published here and are not any more.
#
# If you followed a link into this repository and it no longer resolves, this file
# says where the thing went. Every entry below is a deliberate removal, not an
# accident: one path or path-prefix per line, with the reason above it.
#
# `#` starts a comment. A comment-only file declares nothing.


# ── The ECF conformance corpus is de-versioned ─────────────────────────────────
# `conformance-vectors-v1.cbor` and `conformance-vectors-v1.diag` are now
# `conformance-vectors.cbor` and `conformance-vectors.diag`, in the same directory.
#
# NO VECTOR VALUE CHANGED. The `.cbor` — the artifact an implementation loads — is
# byte-identical across the rename:
#
#     sha256 9695b1f1d939cfdfdd4297f8ad32122d424b1ec180cfae74c92d509d88f7c6dc
#     71 vectors, measured on both sides
#
# The `.diag` moved by two header-comment lines that named the old filename, and by
# nothing else; it is non-normative source, and its digest is recorded in
# `specs/test-vectors/ecf-conformance/CHANGELOG.md` beside the vectors.
#
# The version was in the filename because the corpus had no other place to carry its
# history. It has one now — that CHANGELOG — so the name no longer needs a number,
# and a number in a filename is a pin that silently stops matching.
#
# ⚠ IF YOU VENDOR THIS CORPUS, PIN IT BY DIGEST, NOT BY FILENAME. A copy fetched by
# the old name does not fail loudly; it fails as "file not found", which a check can
# report as nothing-to-do rather than as a miss.
specs/test-vectors/ecf-conformance/conformance-vectors-v1.cbor
specs/test-vectors/ecf-conformance/conformance-vectors-v1.diag


# ── The crypto-agility corpus is de-versioned ──────────────────────────────────
# The same change, one release earlier, to the other corpus:
# `agility-vectors-v1.{cbor,diag}` are now `agility-vectors.{cbor,diag}` and
# `agility-SEEDS.md` is now `SEEDS.md`, all in `specs/test-vectors/crypto-agility/`.
#
# Byte-neutral across the rename:
#
#     sha256 b5484e84dd2cddfa7d3cc8a041deba92cb29615aedb2180e31d8b6910ac5b648
#     10874 bytes, measured on both sides
#
# A reader loses nothing: the same three files are published under the new names,
# `crypto-agility/README.md` points at them, and `crypto-agility/CHANGELOG.md`
# carries the corpus history back to the first byte-pin.
#
# All three are listed even though a rename-detecting diff only ever surfaces the
# `.cbor` as a deletion. This file is the record of what was intended, not a
# transcript of what a diff happened to notice.
specs/test-vectors/crypto-agility/agility-SEEDS.md
specs/test-vectors/crypto-agility/agility-vectors-v1.cbor
specs/test-vectors/crypto-agility/agility-vectors-v1.diag


# ── ENTITY-CORE-MACHINE-SPEC is retired ────────────────────────────────────────
# It was a condensed restatement of the three real specifications, maintained by
# hand. There is no replacement and there deliberately will not be one: a
# restatement ages on every edit to its source, nothing can check a specification
# against a paraphrase of itself, and when two of its sections were finally read
# against the originals both were wrong — one missing a refusal row entirely, one
# still carrying a rule the real spec had corrected two releases earlier.
#
# The specifications in `specs/` are the whole contract. Its two sections anyone
# cited live there:
#
#     §1.8 Entity Fidelity        -> ENTITY-CBOR-ENCODING.md   §5.4
#     §6.4 Connection Error Codes -> ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL.md    §4.7
#
# It also advertised itself as a complete machine-readable specification for
# generating a conforming peer. It never was — the generator that produces the
# cross-language peer cohort reads the real specifications and never read this one.
specs/ENTITY-CORE-MACHINE-SPEC.md


# ── The two authoring standards are single-homed elsewhere ─────────────────────
# `SPECIFICATION-FORMAT` (how a normative specification document is written) and
# `STYLE-NAMING-CONVENTIONS` (identifier naming — and these names are part of the
# wire contract) are published by the `entity-system-architecture` repository, at
# `specs/` in that tree. Read them there.
#
# They are not protocol text. The reader who needs them is someone authoring a
# specification, not someone implementing this one, and they cover documents well
# beyond this repository.
#
# The copies here were deleted because two copies of one standard is the defect.
# Inside a single release cycle SPECIFICATION-FORMAT's copy here had fallen a whole
# section family behind — which made three *correct* citations report as stale — and
# STYLE-NAMING's had forked in both directions at once, so neither side was a
# superset of the other and there was no clean copy to sync from.
specs/SPECIFICATION-FORMAT.md
specs/STYLE-NAMING-CONVENTIONS.md


# ── The rolling status file is no longer published ─────────────────────────────
# `docs/status/STATUS.md` was public at the last release and is not any more, along
# with the dated snapshots beside it.
#
# It is working memory — in-flight measurements, half-verified numbers, notes to the
# next person picking the work up — and its whole value is being written without an
# audience. Publishing it is what turns it into prose for one.
#
# Nothing a reader came for is in it: `README.md` says what this is, `CHANGELOG.md`
# says what changed, and the specifications are the artifact.
#
# A prefix, not one file: nothing under `docs/status/` publishes.
docs/status
